 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 1,
  "id": "3819e2fc",
  "text": "This comes up the river with the grain boats. Every house on the Nile has one like it, and every house is right to.",
  "start": 13.9,
  "end": 21.62
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 2,
  "id": "553c5378",
  "text": "Flatwoven, tight, and the red is a real madder red. Simple work done properly.",
  "start": 23.44,
  "end": 31.1
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 3,
  "id": "ec16b5dc",
  "text": "Nothing to wear down. Beat it, wash it, turn it over. It will outlast the floor.",
  "start": 34.76,
  "end": 39.92
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 4,
  "id": "c99be0f5",
  "text": "A sailor's rug. They buy these at Ismailia and carry them round the world rolled in a sea chest.",
  "start": 45.16,
  "end": 53.58
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 5,
  "id": "21d37168",
  "text": "The hooks and lozenges are old village patterns. Look at the little gaps where the colours meet: woven by hand.",
  "start": 59.32,
  "end": 68.84
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 6,
  "id": "7892d765",
  "text": "Flat and tightly packed, no pile to wear through. Feet, chair legs, years of traffic: it takes all of it and shows nothing.",
  "start": 74.04,
  "end": 86.32
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 7,
  "id": "5db37417",
  "text": "A family rug from the Delta. The kind a man buys when he marries and keeps until his grandchildren sit on it.",
  "start": 88.78,
  "end": 97.32
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 8,
  "id": "e1ebaa5c",
  "text": "Turkish knots, good thick wool, and the medallions are drawn by eye, not from a cartoon. You can see the weaver's hand.",
  "start": 100.82,
  "end": 111.6
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 9,
  "id": "d9a333fc",
  "text": "Thick village wool. Children, guests, feasts: it takes all of it.",
  "start": 113.8,
  "end": 119.88
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 10,
  "id": "aa2745f6",
  "text": "Your father kept this one back. He said it was woven by a family in the Fayoum who put a little camel in every rug they made. There, in the corner.",
  "start": 122.9,
  "end": 133.2
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 11,
  "id": "8fe182b9",
  "text": "Coarse wool, and the red changes shade across the field. That is abrash: a new batch of dye. Machines never do it.",
  "start": 136.26,
  "end": 147.08
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 12,
  "id": "c6005f04",
  "text": "It has already lived twenty years on a stone floor. It will live twenty more.",
  "start": 149.74,
  "end": 154.78
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 13,
  "id": "ca4c6f9d",
  "text": "The dealers call these Red Medina. It is a market name, not a town. The red is what people come back for.",
  "start": 158.8,
  "end": 166.62
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 14,
  "id": "b4b65f74",
  "text": "The medallion is an old Anatolian shape, drawn clean. Good red, good blue, and no chemical orange.",
  "start": 169.18,
  "end": 179.62
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 15,
  "id": "889b05ed",
  "text": "Dark red forgives everything: coffee, feet, the street. It will look better in ten years.",
  "start": 185.36,
  "end": 191.88
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 16,
  "id": "0daf9bcc",
  "text": "For a corridor. You walk in along a line of palm trees, like the road to Saqqara.",
  "start": 197.28,
  "end": 202.92
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 17,
  "id": "b1612f51",
  "text": "Every palm is slightly different. The weaver was counting in her head, not from a paper.",
  "start": 205.86,
  "end": 211.22
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 18,
  "id": "75fcdd2a",
  "text": "Runners take the most feet in the house. This one has the wool for it.",
  "start": 214.06,
  "end": 218.1
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 19,
  "id": "3a08c7b5",
  "text": "From the moulid at Tanta. The women there buy light rugs for their receiving rooms and fight over the good ones.",
  "start": 221.74,
  "end": 229.64
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 20,
  "id": "5dd45c11",
  "text": "A pale field is hard to weave. One dirty hand and it shows. This one is clean.",
  "start": 233.06,
  "end": 239.94
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 21,
  "id": "fd987a34",
  "text": "For a receiving room, not a kitchen. Shoes off, and it will stay beautiful.",
  "start": 242.16,
  "end": 246.6
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 22,
  "id": "7ef1611e",
  "text": "The shipping agents at Port Said put these in their offices. A French captain tried to buy this one off the quay.",
  "start": 248.66,
  "end": 255.86
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 23,
  "id": "299184f5",
  "text": "Wool on a cotton warp, so it lies flat. The octagons are a trade pattern every weaver from Tabriz to Smyrna knows.",
  "start": 258.24,
  "end": 267.92
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 24,
  "id": "be1e0e5a",
  "text": "Indigo does not fade. Put it in the sun and it stays blue.",
  "start": 270.3,
  "end": 274.16
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 25,
  "id": "d338f65e",
  "text": "Cairo work. The old Mamluk weavers used stars like these five hundred years ago, and a few workshops near the Khan still do.",
  "start": 276.54,
  "end": 286.94
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 26,
  "id": "96f6527c",
  "text": "Fine wool and a restrained palette, only a few colours. The stars are laid out by count, row by row.",
  "start": 289.7,
  "end": 297.56
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 27,
  "id": "aae0721a",
  "text": "Fine wool is stronger than it looks. In a sitting room it will outlast the house.",
  "start": 299.04,
  "end": 306.28
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 28,
  "id": "462e972e",
  "text": "The orange growers of Jaffa buy these for their new houses. The rosettes in the border, they say, are oranges.",
  "start": 308.0,
  "end": 319.38
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 29,
  "id": "37487d42",
  "text": "Three medallions in a column, and the border never breaks at the corners. That takes planning.",
  "start": 321.1,
  "end": 328.02
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 30,
  "id": "0013ab5b",
  "text": "Cotton warp, good wool. A family rug that can take a family.",
  "start": 329.32,
  "end": 333.22
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 31,
  "id": "7f5e6787",
  "text": "This one has travelled. It crossed the Lebanon on a mule, and someone mended it in Damascus. See the new wool at the edge.",
  "start": 335.16,
  "end": 344.05
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
  "n": 32,
  "id": "06a59367",
  "text": "Two traditions in one rug: Syrian stripes, Anatolian stars. Nobody knows exactly where it was woven.",
  "start": 351.14,
  "end": 357.08
 },
 {
  "sec": "F",
